A||||6||JohnF||'88 Rear Caliper Source?||||5||
Z||000000||JohnF||11-18-2001||07:56 AM||||Seems like a good place ot another brake question.<P>Yesterday, I rebuilt/replaced my '88 coupe's brakes all around.  Noticed I am going to need rear calipers (one is seeping).<P>Any sources that don't require my first born(Fiero store is $140 ea.)?<P>And , yes, I want to keep my emergency brake sevicable.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-18-2001||10:22 AM||||Autozone- Pep Boys- most major parts stores can get them. Rears are about $90 or so||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sage||11-18-2001||10:29 AM||||We bought some from the local Advance Auto Parts store last year.  It was just the "bridge"(has new piston and sleeves in it) and an exchange program, but they were under $45.00 each, as I recall.  Pontiac dealer wanted over $60.00 just for a sleeve kit!||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||11-18-2001||03:45 PM||||I just called Checker to get mine replaced. They cost $129.00 loaded and $79.99 unloaded. The loaded ones come with sliders, springs and even pads (I asked the guy all these questions and he said it "should" come with everything.) The core on the loaded one was 30 bucks and the core on the unloaded one was 79 bucks. I plan to order the loaded one to see what it contains. If it has new sliders and boots, it would be worth it to me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||11-18-2001||03:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by SCCA FIERO:<BR><B>I just called Checker to get mine replaced. They cost $129.00 loaded and $79.99 unloaded. The loaded ones come with sliders, springs and even pads (I asked the guy all these questions and he said it "should" come with everything.) The core on the loaded one was 30 bucks and the core on the unloaded one was 79 bucks. I plan to order the loaded one to see what it contains. If it has new sliders and boots, it would be worth it to me.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Loaded calipers come with everything......it's as simple as unbolting your old one and bolting in the new one.  <P>I bought a pair of Raybestos loaded front calipers last year and they were 100% complete (caliper, sliders, pads, etc.).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnF||11-18-2001||03:54 PM||||Well now, we've got anywhere from $140 to $130 to $90 (loaded, I presume?  I never heard that term used).  <P>BTW - Neither Autozone, nor NAPA list these on their weeb site.<P>Tthanks for the info.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||9
Z||000006||mrfiero||11-18-2001||04:05 PM||||Checker Auto/Parts America/schucks/Kragen, etc. sells loaded rear calipers for about $100, depending on the brand.  I prefer Raybestos and they are $108 apiece loaded.<P>Go here:  <A HREF="http://www.partsamerica.com/SelectParts.asp?SourceArea=SHOP&PartType=5003&PTSet=A&Description=Brake+Hydraulics&SourcePage=PartTypes&SearchFor=Caliper" TARGET=_blank>http://www.partsamerica.com/SelectParts.asp?SourceArea=SHOP&PartType=5003&PTSet=A&Description=Brake+Hydraulics&SourcePage=PartTypes&SearchFor=Caliper</A> <P>For a listing of their calipers||||reg||1
